 Utilities priced a C$1.1B bond \"comfortably\", so at least one issuer has investment-grade access.\n- I found no data on leverage, coverage or ratings, so I couldn't test how much more debt each utility can carry.\n\n**Equity**\n- Pellham Power withdrew a C$2.4B equity offering because demand fell short.\n- Two other utility share offerings were cut back in size.\n- The sources give no share prices, valuation multiples or dilution figures. The failed and shrunken deals suggest that issuing at current prices is too hard to place or too dilutive.\n\n**By utility**\n- **Norvane:** Its 2027 capex rises 12%. It can probably fund most of that with bonds at higher spreads.\n- **Pellham:** Its 2027 capex rises 18%. It has already cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and deferred two substation programmes \"until financing improves\". Debt alone probably can't replace the lost C$2.4B of equity without a credit cost, so it will likely fund only a scaled-back 2027 programme. That Pellham chose to cut spending rather than issue more debt suggests balance-sheet limits. That is my inference, not something the sources state.\n\n**Knock-on effect:** Pellham is Caldria's largest single buyer of grid equipment, so its cuts weaken 2027 demand for suppliers.\n\n**What would settle it:** absolute capex totals, ratings and FFO/debt for each utility, and the size of Pellham's funding gap.\n\nSources: utility-capex-2027, utility-credit, pellham-offering. The debt findings are in n7.r1 and the equity findings in n8.r1.","ttft_ms":5033,"type":"result","duration_ms":43400,"uuid":"f4818164-8fb3-4ed6-a5fa-a49ec5b027cb","ttft_stream_ms":4809,"time_to_request_ms":140,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":9,"system_prompt":5,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":38,"input_hooks":7,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":7,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":11,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":5,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":5,"model_config_wait":4,"autocompact":